I'm Angela Eagle I'm the Labour MP for  Wallace II we've just written a book the  new serfdom what conservative ideas are  you attacking in the book well the idea  of the book is that it demonstrates the  often hidden influence of a philosopher  and economist called Frederick Hayek who  was mrs. Thatcher's guru who decided  that really the only way of making any  decision in in any country was to use  the price mechanism if you were to  preserve Liberty and yeah the very  extreme view of what I've called market  fundamentalism which has come to be the  dominant idea in the way our economies  develop since mrs. Thatcher's time and I  think it's actually led to a different  kind of serfdom than the one he was  warning about in the 1940s so why is  this influence hidden because I think  it's become all pervasive and a lot of  the assumptions that mrs. Thatcher kind  of injected into the way that we do  things in politics have just become  accepted and actually these ideas when  you examine them are completely  ludicrous and some of them are even  offensive so the price mechanism doesn't  work in many many instances for example  it has nothing to say about the needs or  requirements of anyone who doesn't have  any money  because their needs and just aren't  taken account of in any market markets  fail in many ways one of the other  things that Frederick Hayek felt you  needed in a society was really that to  recognize that people were motivated by  their own selfishness and greed  effectively and to maximize their own  utility and that if everybody did that  then that would provide the best  solution to any system we'll actually as  Democratic Socialist I believe that we  that's not true that people are also  altruistic and there are collective  beliefs and goods in society that I want  to see provided for everybody so I don't  think I've spent my life just utilize  you been sort of motivated by my own  selfish greed in fact I think a lot of  people in society give back to others  and they empathize and identify with  others  Fredrik Iook also really was asserting  the for a society to work properly and  from markets to work properly  it had to be unequal so that those who  were much poorer would strive and work  harder again I think that this is  profoundly wrong and immoral way of  looking at things and so the book is  about how those ideas have almost become  like wallpaper the background against  which every other decision is made and  what I'm saying is we should look at  these ideas for what they are and see  how much they've ruined our world the  market fundamentalist ideas don't take  account of social justice they don't  bring about inclusive growth which  includes everybody and they certainly  don't do a lot to ensure that the growth  that we have is sustainable in  environmental terms so it's time to move  on and so he was fighting against one  type of serf and you said yes his book  was called the road to serfdom and it  was written in 1943 it's very famous  rant against socialism of any kind and  he thought that Clement Attlee was the  most appalling person and he thought he  saw that the collective work that was  going on to fight Hitler during the  Second World War and thought that this  would lead to serfdom and my arguments  are that the values that he set out in  his book which have been put into effect  by Margaret Thatcher and not mitigated  enough subsequently have actually led to  a different kind of serfdom in our labor  market where people are expected to work  for very low pay  creating a society where we've got food  banks and where we can't just satisfy  the basic necessities for a lot of our  people for example for housing for  access to food and all of those things  and so does that mean that you because  you've talked about the fundamentalism  of economic theory and and often you  know the conservatives present  themselves as the only people who can  run the economy and they also present  these ideas as if there's some kind of  natural law and I'm saying actually no  we can make different choices that are  more collective and actually democratic  socialism gives us different sorts of  values and so the values I set out for a  different sort of society a democratic  socialist society are democracy Liberty  cooperation internationalism and  equality I think you can create a better  society if you put those values into  effect how do you hope for these values  to kind of materialize what kind of  policies do you hope to to be adopted in  the I suggest various different things  because it's quite a wide ranging it  dips into loads of different things  without without going into huge detail  about any but I think we need a more  empowering state we need to have the  idea of having a state that allows  people to develop their full potential  rather than seeing it as wholly bad  which the the conservative view usually  is you need the smallest possible state  but I think we need an empowering one I  think we need an an ethical taxation  system so I think people need to  disapprove actively of those that try to  avoid paying their fair share of taxes I  think we need things like wealth taxes  because at the moment in our system we  have one of the most unequal  distributions of wealth and income that  we've had in our history and it's  getting worse so I think you have to  look at things like wealth taxes closing  tax havens down so that people cannot  escape by sending their wealth offshore  participating in the collective  provision of goods and services like  roads and a judicial system but not  paying anything so um one fear with them  and taxing wealthy people in a global  economy is that they might just move  there and they might just give up on  Britain and move elsewhere and pay taxes  or not pay taxes elsewhere does that  mean you have to have an international  solution to the fact that large amounts  of wealth now are being put offshore so  people can escape their obligations to  the countries that they live and work in  and I think we've got to stop that  happening and you can do that with  international agreements to close some  of these these dark spaces down and I  think that people should as citizens of  a particular country be proud to pay  their fair share of taxes to enable  provisions to be made for everybody it's  part of being a social being and I think  that human beings are social beings that  have moral obligations to each other not  just their own selfish desires and I  think that's the difference the  philosophical difference between the  Frederick Hayek disciples and those of  us who want a democratic form of  socialism and more more collective  provision where do you hope for the  future of the Labour Party now it seems  like we're at a crossroad I want us to  be in government putting some of these  ideas into effects I think I'm a product  of previous Labour government's I was  the first woman in my family ever to get  the chance to go to university and I  went to Oxford it gave me chances and  opportunities that many many  working-class people got because of  previous labour governments and I want  to see those chances and opportunities  available to all regardless of their  social standing or wealth in the future  as well I think by working together we  can actually  transform people's lives and prospects  and you do that in in my belief with  labour governments and given that we've  just found out the results from the  European elections what does that mean  about Labor's position on brexit well  first of all I think brexit is a  profoundly reactionary thing I think  that those that push for brexit are  trying to create a circumstance where we  separate from the European Union and all  of the treaties that we've signed up to  which put a floor under workers rights  and environmental rights so that we can  have a kind of turbocharged Thatcherism  on the edge of Europe well this is a  reactionary project that I don't think  we should be supporting and we've got to  do our best to ensure that we mitigate  the effects so far we're in a situation  where I think that we need a clearer  position on how we move forwards we've  clearly fallen between two stools and  the election results last night which  are extremely disappointing  but some people want to Tober  Thatcherism to come out of brexit others  just want a kind of sovereignty right  the electorate of the brexit party is  not necessarily a factor right no no but  the people that are driving it I've got  that agenda and they told quite a lot of  lies in the election the original  referendum they said we'd get all this  extra money for the NHS and they did a  very anti-immigrant kind of pitch so  they were trading on resentment and fear  I think obviously we can be sovereign  and we used to hold our own in a  sovereign way in the way in which we  dealt with our partners in the European  Union if you trade a bit of sovereignty  you can have a bigger effect on the  global stage if you're talking about 250  million people rather than 60 million so  it's about how we amplify our  global influence and I think turning our  backs on our nearest neighbors and  somehow signaling that we don't want  anything to do with them it's not the  best way to try to deal with some of the  problems that the world is facing at the  moment final question you entered  politics as a young woman what's your  advice to young people thinking about  going into politics now well I mean you  have to know why you're doing it and  that's not about I don't think a career  it's about remaking our society to fit  the values that you share with others in  whichever political party are in  obviously women have a tougher time than  men in the way that I think that they're  treated by the media and all of that but  we've made huge strides in the 28 years  I've been in Parliament when I first was  elected in 1992 there are only 60 women  out of 650 in parliament and we've got a  lot more than that now so progress does  happen and I'd say try to be part of  that change thank you very much thank  you for more debates talks and  interviews subscribe today to the  institutes of arson ideas at iai TV  you

